  "id": "DrugBank_DB00958 - MONDO_2752",
  "verbalization": "Carboplatin, a platinum-based chemotherapy agent, is implicated in the development of ovarian adenocarcinoma through a series of indirect pathways. The evidence suggests that Carboplatin may act as a potential therapeutic agent for ovarian adenocarcinoma through its ability to induce the expression of topotecan, a chemotherapy drug that is known to target cancer cells by inhibiting topoisomerase II. This mechanism is supported by the observed indications of Carboplatin in ovarian carcinosarcoma and ovarian endometrioid adenocarcinoma, both of which are histologically distinct but share a common pathway of dependence on topotecan for their progression. The final scores from the paths indicate a moderate level of confidence in the relationship, suggesting that Carboplatin may influence the progression of ovarian adenocarcinoma through a shared mechanism involving topotecan. The integration of these pathways highlights the potential for Carboplatin to be repurposed in the treatment of ovarian adenocarcinoma, particularly in cases where topotecan is already being used.",
